Do not leak a GIL state when shutting Python down.

`PythonInitializer.Shutdown` acquires the GIL into a local that is never
disposed, so `Py.GILState`'s finalizer later runs on the GC's finalizer thread
and throws

    GIL must always be released, and it must be released from the same thread
    that acquired it

which aborts the process after the backtest has finished (exit code 134). It
does not show up under the container's Python 3.11, but it does under the
Python 3.12 used here. `PythonEngine.Shutdown` takes the GIL itself, so the
extra acquisition is redundant.

--- a/Common/Python/PythonInitializer.cs
+++ b/Common/Python/PythonInitializer.cs
@@ -82,7 +82,6 @@
 
                 try
                 {
-                    var pyLock = Py.GIL();
                     Log.Trace($"PythonInitializer.Shutdown(): calling engine shutdown...");
                     PythonEngine.Shutdown();
                 }
